{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The parent maps the supplied states to dk_dt only through dk_dt=-epsilon, so for any observed epsilon the target slope is fixed and independent of how depleted k has become or how strong the delayed cascade state q is.",
      "child_relation": "Refine the dissipation production/decay law so that the observed epsilon trajectory develops a stronger low-k collapse after prolonged decay, via an additional state-dependent slowdown or shutdown of cascade-fed dissipation as the large-eddy reservoir is exhausted.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "k",
        "epsilon",
        "q"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across trajectories that pass through similar epsilon but different k/q combinations during decay, the child produces sharper late-time bending of epsilon(t) and therefore a different subsequent dk_dt history than the parent’s single-phase decay can match with one shared parameter set."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "cascade_ratio",
        "expression": "q*k/epsilon",
        "role": "Measures spectral disequilibrium between delayed cascade transfer and current dissipation level"
      },
      {
        "name": "energy_depletion",
        "expression": "k/(k_feed_scale + k)",
        "role": "Measures how strongly the remaining large-scale energy can still sustain dissipation production"
      },
      {
        "name": "memory_loading",
        "expression": "q/(q_saturation + q)",
        "role": "Measures saturation of the cascade-memory contribution to dissipation production"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable during free decay once k becomes comparable to or smaller than k_feed_scale while q remains finite from earlier cascade loading, so the system enters a late-decay regime not captured by a single smooth phase.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the mechanism appears as mild extra bending when decay approaches the feed scale; in holdout, the same mechanism is amplified over longer observation windows where k stays low for longer and parent single-phase dynamics cannot preserve both early and late behavior with one parameter set.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit where the added state dependence becomes negligible, e.g. when k is large compared with the new attenuation scale or when the refinement strength tends to zero.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current cascade-feed closure assumes one fixed functional phase for dissipation production throughout decay, even though exhaustion of energy-containing eddies should further weaken transfer to the dissipative range beyond the present simple feed factor.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The effectiveness of cascade-fed dissipation production is fully captured by the existing saturating factor k/(k_feed_scale+k) with no additional late-decay change in shape.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Progressive shutdown of cascade effectiveness in strongly depleted turbulence, producing extra curvature in dissipation decay as the large-eddy reservoir becomes too weak to sustain the earlier transfer pathway.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "k",
      "epsilon",
      "q",
      "k_feed_scale",
      "q_saturation"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent’s single-phase assumption for how cascade memory feeds dissipation. Hold the scenario assumptions of homogeneous freely decaying turbulence with no forcing, shear, buoyancy, or transport. Vary only the elapsed decay stage along the admissible trajectory generated from the given initial conditions; this changes k and q while preserving the same model parameters and physical setup. In the parent, once epsilon is observed, dk_dt is fixed, and the only route to richer target behavior is through how epsilon evolves. That evolution currently uses one smooth saturating feed factor in k and one saturation in q, so the dissipation-production response remains a single-phase function of depletion. A scientifically natural gap is that very low-k turbulence should exhibit an additional weakening of effective transfer from energy-containing eddies to the dissipative range, causing a stronger late-time collapse of epsilon than the same law that fit early and intermediate decay. This is observable when k drops into the range of k_feed_scale and q is still non-negligible because of memory; then similar epsilon values can occur at different decay stages, but the subsequent epsilon slope and hence future dk_dt differ. A single shared parent parameter set can shift the timing or overall gain of production and decay, but it cannot independently match both the earlier moderate-curvature regime and the later sharper downturn if both occur within the observed trajectory. No new state is required because the effect can be expressed as a refined state dependence of the existing cascade-feed term rather than a distinct stored physical variable. Limitation: if observations never reach low-k conditions relative to the activation scale, the refinement would be weakly identifiable."
  },
